hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to talk  about when not to use bacteriostatic  drugs so just to review really quickly  there are two major categories of drugs  with in respect to treating bacterial  infections there are drugs that are  bacteriostatic meaning that they prevent  the multiplication the growth of the  bacteria in the patient and that's  represented here by bacteria that have  been lassoed that have rope tied around  them to keep them from going anywhere  keep them from doing anything mainly  keep them from from replicating the  other major class of antibiotic is  called bactericidal so that is when the  bacteria are actually killed by the drug  so these are dead bacteria so now with  that in mind there are times when a  bactericidal drug that actually kills  the bacteria is essential for a good  outcome in a patient so for example when  the patient's own immune system can't  kill bacteria very well such as in cases  of immunodeficiency so think about maybe  people who are HIV positive of a  suppressed immune system people who are  undergoing radiation treatments and  chemotherapy for cancer that have had  their immune system weakened by that the  elderly the very young people who are  that have some kind of immune system  disease think like severe combined  immunodeficiency syndrome things like  that  these are all examples of  immunodeficiency when a patient's own  immune system just can't take care of  the bacteria by themselves so impact  Iria static drug that just prevents the  most location and rates for the immune  system to kick in that's not going to  help these people and so a bactericidal  drug is really critical in that case and  a bacteriostatic drug would not be  effective there's also cases when a  patient would die or have major brain  damage before a bacteriostatic drug can  be effective so basically if the  infection is too severe or or think  about things like encephalitis also  meningitis cases of sepsis these are all  very very serious types of infections  where a bacteriostatic drug just can't  be effective quickly enough to save the  patient's life and so that's why the  bacteria's bactericidal drug would be  required in those cases another type of  case or you really need one of these  bactericidal drugs it actually kills the  bacteria itself is when the site of  infection is poorly accessible to the  immune system so when cells of the  immune system like phagocytic cells  cannot access the site of infection so  for example endocarditis when you have  an infection of heart valves this is  another that this is an example of when  you have an infection in a place where  the immune system just can't get to it  and with a bacteriostatic drugs just  prevent multiplication for a while in  order to let the immune system take care  of the infection but if the immune  system cells can't get there then once  the patient stops taking the bacterias  out of drugs the infection is going to  continue and put it at full strength so  that's why again this bactericidal drug  is really important in a case like this  so you might be asking yourself well  when would you want to use these  bacteriostatic drugs well there are  certain bacterias that drugs that target  very specific bacteria  so if your doctor can diagnose you with  a very specific pathogen sometimes there  are bacteriostatic drugs that are  designed for that pathogen in particular  that would be a good thing to take also  it's possible that sometimes it may be  the only drug available due to  antibiotic resistance and that's pretty  scary antibiotic resistance is a really  problem is getting bigger and bigger  over time where fewer and fewer of our  drugs are working against various  bacterial pathogens because so many of  them have become resistant and so if a  bacteria and the bacteria aside drug is  the only one that's going to work then  that's just all there is and you have to  hope that the patient's immune system is  going to be effective enough to take  those bacteria that are being held in  check by the bacteria side drug and  actually kill them another example is  when it's used in conjunction with  another drug for synergistic effects and  what this means is that sometimes you  can have two bacteriostatic drugs that  are bacteriostatic when they're applied  separately but when they're applied  together they're actually bactericidal  so that's an example of when you can use  two drugs at the same time to treat the  same infection and get a more powerful  effect and a better outcome so that's  another time when bacteriostatic drugs  might be used if you want a more  in-depth explanation of the differences  between bacteriostatic and bactericidal  then please check out my video on  bacteriostatic versus bactericidal drugs  and if you want to learn a bit more  about the antibiotic resistance problems  that we are encountering as a human  population in our in our  sort of medical world and please check  out my video on antibiotic resistance  bacteria and that's it for today thanks  for watching biology professor
 

